This is a classic financial aggregation task. To make this production-ready and compliant with SOLID principles, we will separate concerns strictly:
 * Entity Layer (SRP): Represents the database state only.
 * Repository Layer (ISP): Handles data access and aggregation queries.
 * Service Layer (OCP): Contains business logic (converting DB results to DTOs).
 * Controller Layer (SRP): Handles HTTP requests/responses.
 * DTOs: Decouples the API contract from the database schema.
 * Global Exception Handling: Centralizes error management.
Here is the complete Spring Boot implementation.
1. Project Structure & Dependencies
Ensure you have spring-boot-starter-data-jpa, spring-boot-starter-web, and lombok in your pom.xml.
2. The Entity (Data Layer)
We map the exact columns provided. We use BigDecimal for PROFIT_LOSS to avoid floating-point precision errors (crucial for financial data).
package com.company.finance.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Data;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;

@Entity
@Table(name = "P_L_TRANSFER")
@Data
@NoArgsConstructor
@AllArgsConstructor
public class ProfitLossTransfer {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id; // Assuming a surrogate key exists, otherwise use composite key

    @Column(name = "TRANSFER_DATE", nullable = false)
    private LocalDate transferDate;

    @Column(name = "BRANCH_CODE", nullable = false, precision = 5)
    private Integer branchCode;

    @Column(name = "CIRCLE_BRANCH_CODE", nullable = false, precision = 5)
    private Integer circleBranchCode;

    @Column(name = "PROFIT_LOSS", precision = 25, scale = 4)
    private BigDecimal profitLoss;
}

3. The DTO (Data Transfer Object)
This object defines exactly what the API consumer will see, hiding the complexity of the raw entity.
package com.company.finance.dto;

import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Data;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;

@Data
@AllArgsConstructor
@NoArgsConstructor
public class ProfitLossSummaryDto {
    private LocalDate transferDate;
    private Integer circleBranchCode;
    private BigDecimal totalProfitLoss;
}

4. The Repository (Data Access)
We use a JPQL query to handle the GROUP BY logic directly in the database. This is more efficient than fetching all records and grouping in Java.
Note: Since the summary includes TRANSFER_DATE, we must Group By both TRANSFER_DATE and CIRCLE_BRANCH_CODE to maintain data integrity.
package com.company.finance.repository;

import com.company.finance.dto.ProfitLossSummaryDto;
import com.company.finance.model.ProfitLossTransfer;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

import java.util.List;

@Repository
public interface ProfitLossRepository extends JpaRepository<ProfitLossTransfer, Long> {

    // JPQL Constructor Expression to map directly to DTO
    @Query("SELECT new com.company.finance.dto.ProfitLossSummaryDto(" +
           "p.transferDate, p.circleBranchCode, SUM(p.profitLoss)) " +
           "FROM ProfitLossTransfer p " +
           "GROUP BY p.circleBranchCode, p.transferDate " +
           "ORDER BY p.transferDate DESC")
    List<ProfitLossSummaryDto> findProfitLossSummaryGroupedByCircle();
}

5. Service Layer (Business Logic)
This layer acts as a facade. If business rules change (e.g., applying tax logic before returning), we change it here without breaking the controller or repository.
package com.company.finance.service;

import com.company.finance.dto.ProfitLossSummaryDto;
import com.company.finance.repository.ProfitLossRepository;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

import java.util.List;

public interface ProfitLossService {
    List<ProfitLossSummaryDto> getCircleWiseSummary();
}

@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class ProfitLossServiceImpl implements ProfitLossService {

    private final ProfitLossRepository repository;

    @Override
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List<ProfitLossSummaryDto> getCircleWiseSummary() {
        List<ProfitLossSummaryDto> summary = repository.findProfitLossSummaryGroupedByCircle();
        
        if (summary.isEmpty()) {
            throw new com.company.finance.exception.ResourceNotFoundException("No profit/loss data found for summary generation.");
        }
        
        return summary;
    }
}

6. Global Exception Handling
We capture errors globally to return consistent JSON error responses, preventing stack traces from leaking to the client.
package com.company.finance.exception;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ControllerAdvice;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

// 1. Custom Exception Class
class ResourceNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
    public ResourceNotFoundException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

// 2. Global Handler
@ControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<Object> handleResourceNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        Map<String, Object> body = new HashMap<>();
        body.put("timestamp", LocalDateTime.now());
        body.put("status", HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND.value());
        body.put("error", "Not Found");
        body.put("message", ex.getMessage());
        return new ResponseEntity<>(body, HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<Object> handleGeneralException(Exception ex) {
        Map<String, Object> body = new HashMap<>();
        body.put("timestamp", LocalDateTime.now());
        body.put("status", HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR.value());
        body.put("error", "Internal Server Error");
        body.put("message", "An unexpected error occurred processing the summary.");
        return new ResponseEntity<>(body, HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR);
    }
}

7. Controller (Presentation Layer)
Clean and minimal. It delegates purely to the service.
package com.company.finance.controller;

import com.company.finance.dto.ProfitLossSummaryDto;
import com.company.finance.service.ProfitLossService;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

import java.util.List;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/finance")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class ProfitLossController {

    private final ProfitLossService profitLossService;

    @GetMapping("/summary/circle-wise")
    public ResponseEntity<List<ProfitLossSummaryDto>> getCircleWiseSummary() {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(profitLossService.getCircleWiseSummary());
    }
}

Why this is "Production Ready"
 * BigDecimal: Used for PROFIT_LOSS instead of Double. In banking/finance, Double causes rounding errors (e.g., 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000004).
 * DTO Projection: We did not return the Entity. We returned a DTO. This prevents "LazyInitializationException" and accidental data leakage (like internal IDs).
 * Repository Aggregation: We used SUM() inside the database. Fetching 1,000,000 rows to Java and looping through them to sum them up is slow and memory-intensive. Database aggregation is highly optimized.
 * Lombok: Used to reduce boilerplate code (@Data, @RequiredArgsConstructor).
 * Interface-Based Service: ProfitLossService is an interface. This allows you to swap implementations later (e.g., for unit testing) without breaking the controller.
